WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.810 --> 00:00:08.160
This is the first direct image ever taken of a black hole, courtesy of the Event Horizon Telescope.

00:00:16.510 --> 00:00:21.989
Creating this image required more data than any other scientific experiment in history.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:24.200
So how did they do it?

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:27.680
Up until now most measurements of black holes were indirect,

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:31.840
like this time lapse of stars at the centre of our galaxy.

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:37.620
The stars seem to be orbiting around something... something with a humongous gravitational pull.

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:41.920
Scientists predicted that thing was a black hole.

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:48.120
Astronomers have also seen massive jets of matter emanating from the centres of galaxies,

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:51.840
sometimes much larger than the galaxies themselves.

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:57.280
They predict that the only thing powerful enough to create them would be black holes.

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:03.040
The gravitational waves detected by LIGO in 2015

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:07.800
were the most direct and compelling evidence for black holes to date.

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:12.580
But detecting gravitational waves is sort of like hearing a black hole.

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:16.220
Scientists still wanted to see it.

00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:21.000
But how do you take a picture of a black object against a black background?

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:23.470
The answer: back light it.

00:01:23.470 --> 00:01:30.520
Matter should emit radiation as it heats up while circling the edge of a black hole, the event horizon.

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:32.780
With a big enough telescope

00:01:32.780 --> 00:01:39.780
scientists should be able to use that radiation as a backdrop to see the silhouette of the black hole itself.

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:41.040
But it's tricky.

00:01:41.049 --> 00:01:45.809
Even though the black hole is gigantic and has galactic scale impacts,

00:01:45.809 --> 00:01:50.880
it is so far away, to get an image of high enough resolution,

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:55.086
scientists calculated they'd need a telescope the size of the Earth,

00:01:55.086 --> 00:01:58.000
which is obviously impossible.

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:01.300
Enter the Event Horizon Telescope,

00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:03.700
which is not in fact one telescope,

00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:09.240
but a network of eight observatories spread around the world from Hawaii to Antarctica.

00:02:09.820 --> 00:02:13.800
Each observatory provides a tiny piece of the puzzle.

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:16.640
Take repeated readings as the Earth spins,

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:20.540
and you can capture more angles from each telescope.

00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:23.100
Once you have enough pieces of the puzzle,

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:27.340
algorithms can fill in the gaps and build up the whole picture.

00:02:27.340 --> 00:02:30.300
This process is called interferometry.

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:36.960
The observations used to create this image were made over a few nights in 2017.

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:44.120
Each night each observatory gathered the same amount of data as the Large Hadron Collider does in a year

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:48.600
and it has taken two years to piece it all together.

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:51.947
With this image, we can see a black hole for the first time.

00:02:51.947 --> 00:02:54.340
But it's much more than images.

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:59.100
It provides precise tests of the predictions of general relativity.

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:00.540
And what's more,

00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:06.420
beyond the event horizon is a place where physics as we know it no longer works.

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:10.540
This is the first direct image ever taken of a black hole

00:03:10.540 --> 00:03:14.080
but coded in its pixels could be the answers

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:16.560
to questions about the whole universe.

